\tr{\td{convention equivalence per declaration, lanes D = 128 (in force), 64, 0, unbounded (11:0xZ; before 10:29Z the same with 64 in force)}\td{\strong{EQUIVALENT} on tutorial (195 declarations, 175 accepted), \code{\verb~|bugs/~} (1147, 1136) and \strong{Init (57,938, all accepted; 77.8 M ticks, 63.5 M call events)}: outcome, reason, ticks, call events by op and their sequence, every record word incl. memo words, every run word, the pair entries; no pair-order note; the unbounded lane wrote no frame and never overflowed (64 MiB thread); frames on Init: D128 32,274, D64 180,960, D0 145.5 M}\td{\code{\verb~|machine-conv-{tutorial,bugs,init}.txt~}}}
\tr{\td{frames written under the host depth bound on Init, bound 0.1\% of the calls}\td{D = 64 (the bound until 10:29Z): \strong{not met, 0.234\%} (185,128 frames for 79.2 M call events; 1992 spills; 603 declarations with a frame, one of them with 49\% of the frames). D = 128: 0.041\%, D = 256: 0.017\%; ticks and call events identical under all bounds. On this data M6 set \code{\verb~|Op.HOST_DEPTH = 128~}; \strong{with it the bound is met: 32,274 frames for 63.5 M call events = 0.051\%}}\td{\code{\verb~|machine-frames-init.txt~} (128), \code{\verb~|-init-d128.txt~}, \code{\verb~|-init-d256.txt~} (the D = 64 figures are in the log), \code{\verb~|machine-calls-init.tsv~}}}
\tr{\td{readback of \code{\verb~|whnf~} and \code{\verb~|infer~} against the reference kernel on sampled sub-terms (10:02Z)}\td{\strong{AGREE} on Init: 1486 constants, 5944 sub-terms (1253 proofs): 5944 infer, 4691 whnf, 7240 isDefEq, 11,442 isProp / whnfCore / ensurePi answers, 0 differences, 0 undecided; tutorial (175 constants) and \code{\verb~|bugs/~} (1136 constants, 2 undecided) AGREE too}\td{\code{\verb~|machine-sample-{init,tutorial,bugs}.txt~}}}
\tr{\td{K0 = K1 on values}\td{\strong{EQUIVALENT} on Init against \code{\verb~|facts,tele,code,casetrees~} as published: 1486 constants, 5944 results (DELTA, WHNF, VTYPE fingerprints incl. closure identity; CONV), none equal up to PEEK only, none skipped; tutorial (175 constants) likewise. (M7's own \code{\verb~|M7TierTest.initPrelude~} reported differences at 10:42Z after M2's \code{\verb~|let~} change; this test did not see one on its sample: it applies constants to variables and one-level constructors, M7's test drives block bodies directly)}\td{\code{\verb~|machine-tiers-{init,tutorial}.txt~}}}
\tr{\td{Std (v1 gate corpus), in-process, first run (10:36Z)}\td{\strong{no wrong verdict, not clean}: 100,096 engine checks = 100,088 accepted + 8 declined, all \code{\verb~|E_MEM: run words: 2^28 per session~} after 50-54 M records each; they make the run slow (1.34 G ticks; 943 s in-process against 137 s for the reference engine on the loaded host)}\td{\code{\verb~|machine-diff-std.txt~}}}
\tr{\td{Cedar slice \code{\verb~|cedar-hot-ax~}, \code{\verb~|reachability~}, in-process (10:37Z, 10:39Z)}\td{both \strong{DIFF-CLEAN}: 8064 and 4224 engine checks, all accepted}\td{\code{\verb~|machine-diff-cedar.txt~}, \code{\verb~|machine-diff-reachability.txt~}}}
}

\p{\strong{Cost, stage v0} (\code{\verb~|machine-cost.md~}: launcher \code{\verb~|rlvm-jvm --threads 1~} = HotSpot C2, machine jar on \code{\verb~|RLVM_EXTRA_CP~}, arena-shaped container; best CPU time over the runs of a row, 2-3 runs per measurement):}

\table{
\tr{\th{corpus}\th{machine CPU / wall / RSS / instructions}\th{reference engine}\th{machine / reference (CPU)}\th{machine / gruszka (CPU)}}
\tr{\td{Init}\td{14.96 s / 10.9 s / 516 MiB / 155-160 G}\td{15.92 s / 13.8 s / 1253 MiB / 207.9 G}\td{0.94x}\td{5.3x (2.8 s; gate after step 1: 2.5x)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|cedar-hot-ax~}}\td{8.62 s / 4.7 s / 689 MiB / 87.8 G}\td{6.38 s / 4.6 s / 1024 MiB / 71.0 G}\td{1.35x (slower)}\td{-}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|reachability~}}\td{9.41 s / 7.5 s / 2071 MiB / 76.7 G}\td{11.99 s / 11.3 s / 2669 MiB / 101.1 G}\td{0.78x}\td{-}}
}

\p{The host was shared with the other agents of the wave: CPU time moved by up to 50\% with their load (the reference engine on Init: 23.6 s at load average 150, 15.9 s at 70, the same instruction count). A first statement in the log ("0.63x the reference on Init") compared runs under different load and is withdrawn there. The table keeps every measurement and shows the best per row.}

\p{Found by these tools so far: one bug, in M8's own \code{\verb~|Quote~} (D9: the level substitution reached into the bindings; ten "differences" of the sampler on \code{\verb~|bugs/~}, fixed 09:34Z). No difference in a judgment of the machine, no convention difference, no wrong verdict of the published main tree. (The three "WRONG" files in sandbox reports of 08:55-08:58Z were reference-engine declines counted as SOUNDNESS: D8.)}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. Not done, not verified}

\ul{
\li{\strong{K0 = K1 against a real K1}: \code{\verb~|M8Tiers~} and \code{\verb~|TierEq.tierEquivalence~} run and are tested K0 against K0 and against whatever the switches \code{\verb~|facts,tele,code,casetrees~} do today; they were not validated against an elaborator bug planted on purpose. "Random argument values" are fresh variables and one-level constructor applications of index-free inductive types, not arbitrary closed terms.}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|Quote~} of a PII} is tested through \code{\verb~|INFER~} of two lambdas only; a PII whose body type needs a failing judgment raises \code{\verb~|QuoteFailure~} (untested path beyond the cleanup of binders).}
\li{\strong{Loops that never tick} inside a judgment are only caught by the per-file limit; the hung thread is abandoned (daemon) and dies with the JVM.}
\li{\strong{Native image}: nothing of M8 was run on a native image; the micro-benchmark is JVM only (C2 and Graal JIT).}
\li{The cost table measures the JVM launcher with the machine jar on \code{\verb~|RLVM_EXTRA_CP~}, one worker thread; the H10 inlining flags are not set (M6 has not wired them).}
\li{\code{\verb~|report.sh frames~} keeps one previous call table (\code{\verb~|.prev~}), not a history.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. Wave 6: the gate, the mutants, the scoreboard (2026-11-24, 13:25Z onwards)}

\p{M8 is the gatekeeper and the scorekeeper of the wave. Everything below is test-tree code and scripts; no file of another package was touched. The numbers of a run are in section 8; this section is what exists and why it is built the way it is.}

\subtree{
\title{7.1 Commands}

\table{
\tr{\th{Command}\th{Report}\th{What}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|report.sh gate [--switches S] [--tree SANDBOX] [CORPUS..]~}}\td{\code{\verb~|work/status/machine-gate-NAME.md~} (one page) + \code{\verb~|.problems.txt~}}\td{THE GATE for one switch set (none = the generic tier): (1) every declaration against the reference engine on tutorial, bugs, all 230, Init, Std, reachability, the Cedar slice; (2) tier equivalence: the generic tier gives the same outcome for every declaration (another \em{reason} for the same outcome is a note), and for a set with K1 switches the values of \code{\verb~|M8Tiers~} on the tutorial tests and Init; (3) convention equivalence: host depth 0 and unbounded against \code{\verb~|Op.HOST_DEPTH~}: outcome, reason, ticks, call events by op, the frame trace, after an ACCEPT every record word, run word and pair; (4) the debug audits as two lanes, \code{\verb~|poison,scopeaudit,maskaudit~} and \code{\verb~|memocheck,shortcheck~}: the same outcomes and no \code{\verb~|E_AUDIT~}; (5) the real driver in the same JVM with \code{\verb~|-X engine=machine~} on the 230 tests (1 worker) and the corpora (4 workers): verdict, machine declines, MACHINE-INCOMPLETE, WARM-ANOMALY, disagreements. NAME = \code{\verb~|v0~} or the switches joined by \code{\verb~|+~}; a sandbox gets \code{\verb~|-Mk~}; fewer corpora or parts get \code{\verb~|-partial~} and the page says "A PARTIAL RUN, NOT THE GATE". Exit 1 = a wrong verdict, a difference, a crash or a timeout; declines alone are \code{\verb~|PASS WITH DECLINES~}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|report.sh mutants --switches S1 --switches S2 .. [--corpus NAME] [--limit N] [--launcher-sample N]~}}\td{\code{\verb~|work/status/machine-mutants.md~}}\td{the four mutant corpora (17,775 exports classified by the official checker) per switch set: the final verdict of the real pipeline against the official checker (SOUNDNESS, COMPLETENESS), against the same pipeline with the reference engine, and the machine's own answers: from the driver's statistics (MACHINE-INCOMPLETE, WARM-ANOMALY, disagreements, declines) and, per declaration, in-process against the reference engine (the driver trusts an ACCEPT of the machine: only this sees a wrong one). \code{\verb~|--launcher-sample N~} runs every N-th mutant through the real launcher (a JVM per mutant, \src{scripts/check-light.sh}) and holds the in-process verdict against it}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|ladder.py measure [--sets 'v0;warm,gated,expect;..']~}, \code{\verb~|ladder.py render~}, \code{\verb~|ladder.py status~}}\td{\code{\verb~|work/status/machine-ladder.md~}, \code{\verb~|.tsv~}}\td{THE SCOREBOARD: the machine jar published now (named by the hash of its code, so that a test-only publication is the same jar), CE native image \code{\verb~|-O3~} of the published jars, arena-shaped container, ONE hold of the measurement lock per batch: reference engine, gruszka (its arena run line) and every switch set on Init, Std, the Cedar slice, reachability with 1 and 4 workers: CPU s / wall s / RSS MiB / instructions G, ratios to the reference and to gruszka of the same batch. Each batch is announced in \code{\verb~|work/status/machine-M8.log~}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|big.py [--sets ..] [--corpora ..] [--with-reference]~}}\td{\code{\verb~|work/status/machine-big.md~}, \code{\verb~|.tsv~}}\td{the large corpora (con-leche, cedar, cslib, navier-stokes-euler, mathlib) through the native launcher with \code{\verb~|-X engine=machine~}, 4 workers: \code{\verb~|agree~} = machine accepted == engine checks, nothing rejected or declined, no MACHINE-INCOMPLETE; on a corpus every unit of which the reference engine accepts that is the per-declaration differential (the argument is in the header of the script and of the report)}}
}

\p{\code{\verb~|report.sh~} options since wave 6: \code{\verb~|--jobs N~} (passes / files / mutants at a time, default 4), \code{\verb~|--heap~} (default 6g), \code{\verb~|--levels official|complete~} (the level relation of the reference engine, default official: owner note 11), \code{\verb~|--no-pin~}, \code{\verb~|--no-slot~}. \code{\verb~|report.sh tutorial|bugs|all|conv ..~} run on the same parallel runner.}
}

\subtree{
\title{7.2 How the gate stays inside the heap (the two slow tests that ran out of memory)}

\p{\code{\verb~|M8SlowTest.convAll~} (four machine lanes and the reference kernel in lockstep, 6 GB) died with \code{\verb~|OutOfMemoryError~} on perf/app-lam, gf3-matrix-product, magma-list-deep-n36, magma-list-pair-n21; \code{\verb~|M7HookedTest.all230~} ended perf/loop-unfolded-once as CRASH in its K1 lane (\code{\verb~|OutOfMemoryError~} in \code{\verb~|Machine.growRecords~} under \code{\verb~|Diff.run~}). Causes: (a) lockstep keeps the reference kernel's overlay (1.2 GB used, up to three times that allocated, after app-lam) and every lane's machine alive at the same time; (b) the frame trace was kept as an \code{\verb~|int[]~} per lane and copied per declaration (4 bytes per call event: hundreds of MB for one perf declaration); (c) \code{\verb~|Machine.heapAllows~} counts the free bytes of the young generation, which a large array cannot use (SerialGC: a third of the heap by default), so a growth it allowed could still fail.}

\p{What was built (\code{\verb~|M8Lanes~}, the pass runner; \code{\verb~|M8Harness~} keeps lockstep for the single-file tools):}

\ul{
\li{\strong{passes}: the reference engine runs over the whole file first and its session and context are emptied; then every lane runs alone over the same units and lets go of its machine, session and context when it ends; per unit only words are kept (outcome and reason of admission and engine, ticks, call events and a digest by op, the length and a digest of the trace, the sizes and digests of records, runs, pairs). The comparisons (lane against reference = \code{\verb~|Diff.FileResult~} with the fixture's counting rule; lane against lane = \code{\verb~|CONV~} everything observable, or \code{\verb~|OUTCOME~}) are made from those words. Peak memory is one engine on one declaration. A corpus that is one big file runs its lanes on one thread each (they share the frozen store and the finished environment pass; \code{\verb~|EnvBuilder.advanceTo~} returns at once for decided units).}
\li{\strong{a check the Java heap ends is not a verdict}: \code{\verb~|OutOfMemoryError~} inside \code{\verb~|Session.check~} (and a growth that \code{\verb~|Machine.heapAllows~} refused) is a DECLINE flagged \code{\verb~|memory~}; the lane gets a new machine and context after the unit; the comparisons skip the unit; the file is flagged and \code{\verb~|M8Gate.runAll~} runs it again on its own after everything else. A file that still runs out of heap alone is reported as declined for memory, never as a difference. Tested with a seam (\code{\verb~|Config.simulateHeapExhaustionAtCheck~}).}
\li{\strong{scheduling}: \code{\verb~|jobs~} passes at a time (a semaphore); big files (>= 16 MB) one per 5 GB of heap with their lanes in parallel; heavy files (\code{\verb~|work/m8/heavy.txt~} remembers every file whose lane or reference session held more than 256 MiB or that ran out of heap beside others; without that memory the perf tests) a few at a time, in a heap below 10 GB one at a time after everything else.}
\li{\code{\verb~|report.sh~} runs the JVM with \code{\verb~|-Xmn1g~} so that \code{\verb~|heapAllows~} is right about large arrays.}
\li{\strong{warm sessions} (\code{\verb~|Config.incrementalDigest~}): digesting a 4 MiB session after each of Init's 58,000 declarations would cost more than the checks, so after an ACCEPT only what the declaration added is digested, and the whole session once at every reset and at the end of the file.}
\li{a check that its wall-clock deadline ended (\code{\verb~|Config.checkMillis~}, 300 s in the gate; the host is shared) is a decline and is not compared between lanes (it would be a difference made by the load).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7.3 Deviations (continuing the table of section 3)}

\table{
\tr{\th{\verb~|#~}\th{Where}\th{Deviation}\th{Reason}}
\tr{\td{D16}\td{the gate, the corpus reports}\td{lanes are compared from digests recorded per unit, not on live machines; the first difference is named by kind ("records made: 12 against 13"), the words themselves are shown by \code{\verb~|M8ConventionTest#oneFile~} (lockstep)}\td{memory (7.2)}}
\tr{\td{D17}\td{tier equivalence at declaration level, audit lanes}\td{the same outcome for another reason (another error of a wrong declaration met first under \code{\verb~|expect~}) is counted as a note, not as a difference; under a host convention (CONV) the reason must be identical}\td{found on bad/bugs/rec-of-subst-prop unit 128: generic TYPE_EXPECTED, \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~} APP_TYPE_MISMATCH, the reference rejects too}}
\tr{\td{D18}\td{the gate's part 5, the mutants}\td{the real pipeline is called in the report's JVM (\code{\verb~|rlvm.main.RlvmChecker.checkExport~} by reflection; \code{\verb~|report.sh~} puts the published rlvm-main and rlvm-cli jars behind the tree's classes), not started as a launcher process}\td{17,775 mutants x (sets + 1) runs: a JVM each is about 20 minutes per set, a call is milliseconds. Checked against the launcher by \code{\verb~|--launcher-sample~} and by the baseline table (the in-process reference pipeline must reproduce the published \code{\verb~|testkit judge~} counts)}}
\tr{\td{D19}\td{every report}\td{the reference engine compares universe levels as the official kernel does (\code{\verb~|-Drlvm.levels=official~}; \code{\verb~|--levels complete~} for the old relation)}\td{owner note 11; the machine does since 14:22Z (M1), the published rlvm-ref jar still defaults to \code{\verb~|complete~}, and without this every level corner case would show as "the machine rejects, the reference accepts"}}
\tr{\td{D20}\td{the scoreboard}\td{a machine jar is named by a hash of its classes (names and CRCs), not of the file}\td{a publication of tests only installs a jar with other time stamps and the same code}}
\tr{\td{D21}\td{large corpora}\td{"differential" = the machine's own statistics on a corpus the reference engine accepts unit for unit, not both engines on every unit}\td{it is the same statement at a quarter of the CPU time on a host that was out of memory; \code{\verb~|testkit diff --engine machine~} stays the tool for a corpus with rejections}}
\tr{\td{D22}\td{resources}\td{every report holds a host-wide slot, 4 passes, 6 GB, pinned outside the measurement cpuset}\td{the orchestrator's rule of 14:32Z after the host thrashed; my first full gate (12 passes, 24 GB) was one of the jobs that pushed it into the swap and was killed after 25 minutes without a result}}
}
}
}

